\code{wjp_network()} takes a data frame of nodes (with their coordinates) and a
data frame of weighted edges, and returns a ggplot object with a network
chart following WJP style guidelines: round nodes shaded by their
centrality (or by a value), edges whose width and opacity grow with their
weight, and the name and value of each node printed inside it.
}
\details{
The node layout is taken as given (\code{x}, \code{y}): compute it beforehand with
any graph layout algorithm. The chart keeps the aspect ratio of the layout
(one unit of \code{x} equals one unit of \code{y}) and spans the box of the nodes
plus \code{padding}, so a figure saved with the same aspect ratio has no empty
margins.

\strong{Node fill.} With \code{fill = "centrality"}, each node is shaded by the
sum of the weights of its edges (its strength), rescaled to 0-1 and mixed
between the two \code{colors} in RGB space. With \code{fill = "value"}, the column
\code{node_value} is used instead. The text of each node is white on dark fills
and dark on light fills (\code{label_colour = "auto"}).

\strong{Edges.} The weight of each edge is rescaled to 0-1 (\code{t}). Its width
is \code{edge_width[1] + (edge_width[2] - edge_width[1]) * t^edge_gamma} points
and its opacity \code{edge_alpha[1] + (edge_alpha[2] - edge_alpha[1]) * t}. Both
\code{edge_width} and \code{edge_alpha} also accept a single value or a function of
the raw weights. Edges are drawn from the thinnest to the thickest, below
the nodes.

Node names can contain line breaks (\code{"\\n"}); use \code{ids} when the names drawn
differ from the identifiers used in \code{edges}.
}
